/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - An ISFET is a MOSFET (metal-oxide-semiconductor field-effect transistor) with the metal gate removed.
   - Ions bind to surface sites such as SiOH or TaOH.
   - At 25 °C the pH response follows the Nernst slope: 59.16 mV per pH unit.
   - ΔVG is the gate-voltage shift; ΔpH is the pH change; R is 8.314 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹; T is absolute temperature in K; F is 96485 C mol⁻¹; the sign is set by surface charge and feedback polarity.
   - An enzyme field-effect transistor (EnFET) is the same device with an enzyme layer over the gate.
   - An immunologically sensitive field-effect transistor (IMFET) is an ISFET with an antibody immobilised on the gate or in a membrane over it.
   - Readout uses a constant-current or constant-voltage circuit.
   - Fabrication follows standard CMOS steps.
   - Practical limits are drift, hysteresis, light sensitivity and reference-electrode instability.
*/
