/* book Worked example: Q: A pH ISFET has a Nernst slope of 59.16 mV per pH unit at 25 °C and a subthreshold swing of 70 mV per decade. The sample pH falls from 7.4 to 7.1. (a) Find the gate-voltage shift at constant drain current with a feedback gate. (b) Find the drain-current ratio at constant gate voltage. (c) Explain why an IMFET signal from one electronic charge per bound antigen needs a differential pair and averaging.
   1. ΔpH = 7.1 − 7.4 = −0.3 pH units.
   2. |ΔVG| = 59.16 mV/pH × 0.3 pH = 17.748 mV ≈ 17.7 mV. pH falling raises the surface potential, so the feedback gate moves down by 17.7 mV to hold ID.
   3. At constant VG, use S = 70 mV/decade: ID ratio = 10|ΔV|/S = 1017.7/70 = 100.253 = 1.79.
   4. So ID rises by about 1.8 times at fixed VG; the sign follows the surface-charge direction.
   5. For (c), q = 1.602 × 10−19 C and ΔV = q/C. A 10 µm × 10 µm gate with 10 nm of SiO₂ (relative permittivity 3.9) has C = 8.854 × 10−12 × 3.9 × 10−10 / 10−8 = 0.35 pF, so ΔV ≈ 1.602 × 10−19 / 3.5 × 10−13 = 0.46 µV. A smaller gate has a smaller C and a larger shift, up to a few millivolts for nanoscale devices. One charge on a normal gate is far below drift and noise; a reference ISFET subtracts commo
   Answer: The constant-current gate-voltage shift is 17.7 mV downward, the constant-voltage drain current rises by a factor of about 1.79, and one bound charge shifts a 10 µm gate by only about 0.5 µV. */
