/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A glucose strip is a plastic rectangle with a capillary channel.
   - The meter applies a fixed potential and reads current after a few seconds.
   - GOx oxidises glucose to gluconolactone.
   - Most clinics use these cut-offs: fasting plasma glucose 126 mg/dL (7.0 mmol/L) or more; 2-hour OGTT 200 mg/dL (11.1 mmol/L) or more; random glucose 200 mg/dL or more with symptoms; HbA1c 6.5% or more.
   - glucose (mg/dL) is the concentration in milligrams per decilitre; glucose (mmol/L) is the same concentration in millimoles per litre.
   - A meter reads whole blood from a finger.
   - HbA1c is not a strip reading.
   - A continuous glucose monitor (CGM) puts a glucose oxidase wire under the skin and measures interstitial fluid.
   - Accuracy is reported as MARD, the mean absolute relative difference between sensor and reference readings.
   - Error sources: haematocrit; ascorbic acid and uric acid, which oxidise at the electrode and add current; temperature, which changes enzyme rate and diffusion; altitude; expired strips; and reuse.
*/
