/* book Worked example 2: Q: A CGM is checked against laboratory plasma glucose at four times. Sensor: 100, 150, 80 and 210 mg/dL. Reference: 110, 140, 90 and 200 mg/dL. Find the MARD.
   1. Relative difference = |sensor − reference| ÷ reference for each pair.
   2. |100 − 110| ÷ 110 = 10/110 = 9.09 %; |150 − 140| ÷ 140 = 10/140 = 7.14 %.
   3. |80 − 90| ÷ 90 = 10/90 = 11.11 %; |210 − 200| ÷ 200 = 10/200 = 5.00 %.
   4. MARD = (9.09 + 7.14 + 11.11 + 5.00) ÷ 4 = 32.34 ÷ 4 = 8.1 %.
   Answer: MARD is 8.1 %. The same 10 mg/dL error is a larger share at low glucose (11 % at 90 mg/dL), which is why low readings are confirmed with a finger-stick. */
